# Manim Text and Point Accuracy Rules

## Primary text class — always use `HebrewMathLine`

**Never** use Manim's `Text`, `Tex`, or `MathTex` for content lines.
The only text class in this project is `HebrewMathLine` from `hebrew_math_line.py`.

```python
from hebrew_math_line import HebrewMathLine
```

### Default constructor values (use when user does not specify otherwise)

| Parameter | Default |
|-----------|---------|
| `hebrew_font` | `"Alef"` |
| `font_size` | `36` |
| `color` | `WHITE` (this is already `HebrewMathLine`'s default, but be explicit when the context requires it) |

```python
# Minimal correct example
line = HebrewMathLine(r"טקסט עברי עם $f(x)$", font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef")
```

---

## LaTeX source — always drive content through string arguments

Content **must** be passed as positional string argument(s) to the `HebrewMathLine` constructor.
This is the only mechanism that creates addressable Segments.

### Single-argument form (auto-split on `$…$`)

```python
line = HebrewMathLine(
    r"משפט: אם $f(x)$ רציפה בקטע $[a,b]$",
    font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef",
)
```

### Multi-argument form (explicit segments — preferred when you need per-segment styling)

Each positional string becomes exactly one Segment.
Math strings must be wrapped in `$…$`.

```python
line = HebrewMathLine(
    r"\textbf{משפט:}",   # segment 0 — bold Hebrew text
    r" תהא ",            # segment 1 — plain Hebrew text
    r"$f(x)$",           # segment 2 — math
    r" רציפה בקטע ",     # segment 3 — plain Hebrew text
    r"$[a,b]$",          # segment 4 — math
    font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef",
)
```

### `||` delimiter — splitting a single string into multiple TEXT segments

Within a single text-only string you can use `||` to create independent segments without switching to multi-argument form.

```python
line = HebrewMathLine(r"חלק א ||חלק ב|| $f(x)$", font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef")
```

---

## Segment addressing

After construction, `line[i]` addresses the i-th **submobject**.
Because Hebrew is RTL and segments are reordered after compilation, segment indices
in Python correspond to **visual position** (right → left), NOT parse order.
When you need a specific parse-order segment, use the multi-argument form and count
from zero in the order you declared the arguments.

```python
line[0].set_color(YELLOW)        # first (rightmost) segment
line[1].set_color(ManimColor("#00FFFF"))  # second segment
```

---

## Bold and italic

Apply bold / italic **inside the LaTeX source string** using standard LaTeX commands.
Do **not** try to apply them post-hoc with Python attribute calls.

| Effect | Hebrew/text | Math |
|--------|------------|------|
| Bold | `\textbf{...}` | `\mathbf{...}` |
| Italic | `\textit{...}` | `\mathit{...}` |
| Bold + Italic | `\textbf{\textit{...}}` | `\mathbf{\mathit{...}}` |

```python
# Bold title
title = HebrewMathLine(r"\textbf{משפט דיריכלה}", font_size=60, hebrew_font="Alef")

# Italic math inside a mixed line
line = HebrewMathLine(
    r"הפונקציה ",
    r"$\mathit{f}(x)$",
    r" רציפה",
    font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef",
)
```

---

## Positioning — rules and anti-patterns

### Default positioning approach

Use **`to_edge` + `next_to`** for layout. Do **not** use `move_to` with hardcoded
absolute coordinates unless you have a precise measured value from the `/measure` API.

```python
# Top of the page
line_1 = HebrewMathLine(r"\textbf{כותרת}", font_size=60, hebrew_font="Alef")
line_1.to_edge(UP, buff=0.5)          # flush to top edge

# Next line below, right-aligned (Hebrew convention)
line_2 = HebrewMathLine(r"שורה שנייה", font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef")
line_2.next_to(line_1, DOWN, buff=0.3)
line_2.to_edge(RIGHT, buff=0.3)

# Centered formula
formula = HebrewMathLine(r"$\displaystyle{\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n}$", font_size=36, hebrew_font="Alef")
formula.next_to(line_2, DOWN, buff=0.5)
formula.set_x(0)                       # center horizontally
```

### Precise RTL-aware positioning (when `/measure` data is available)

RTL Hebrew text has a large LaTeX bounding-box (symmetric around center) but visible
ink shifted to the right.  `to_edge` uses the bounding box, not the ink edge.
When the UI provides ink measurements (`ink_right_x`, `ink_left_x`, `ink_top_y`, `ink_bottom_y`),
use `set_x` / `set_y` instead of `to_edge`:

```python
# Flush to top using measured ink height (half_height = height_ink / 2)
line.set_y(config.frame_height / 2 - buff - half_height)

# Flush to right using measured ink half-width
line.set_x(config.frame_width / 2 - buff - ink_half_width)
```

### NEVER do this

```python
# Wrong: absolute move_to without measured values
line.move_to(ORIGIN)           # places at (0,0,0) — screen center, not top
line.move_to([0, 0, 0])        # same problem
```

