## Text segment styling rules (Hebrew and math)

- When the user asks to style only part of a sentence (color/bold/italic), split the source text into
  explicit segments using multi-argument form so the requested fragment can be targeted precisely.
- In Hebrew text, `||` is a delimiter between Hebrew text segments inside a single string.
  Preserve and use it to isolate the exact word/phrase to style.
- Math expressions like `$f(x)$` are naturally separate math segments and can be styled independently.
- If the user asks to style only part of a math expression, split it into multiple `$...$` math
  environments so the relevant part is isolated and addressable.

```python
# Color individual segments after construction
line[0].set_color(ManimColor("#ffff00"))   # bold label
line[1].set_color(WHITE)                   # plain text
line[2].set_color(ManimColor("#00FFFF"))   # math term
```

---

## Detecting math context in user requests

- When a user message contains mathematical notation/formulas, represent them in math mode using
  `$...$` inside the LaTeX source string, not as plain text.
- If a mixed sentence contains both words and formulas, keep words as text and formulas wrapped
  in `$...$` as separate arguments or delimited by `$...$` in a single string.

---

## UI-only editing behavior

- Apply changes only through operations that are visible/available in the user UI flow.
- Do not invent hidden/internal transformations that the user cannot see or control.
- If styling a specific Hebrew word/phrase is requested, ensure segmentation markers (`||` or
  multi-argument split) are present so the exact UI-targetable segment exists.
- Do not assign explicit color/style values unless the user asked for them.
- Keep default styling untouched for all non-targeted segments (including math segments like `$f(x)$`).
- If a requested style cannot be applied through existing UI operations, ask for clarification.

---

## Text formatting workflow order

- When a request includes both text creation and formatting requirements, first create the base
  `HebrewMathLine` with the correct LaTeX source and segment split.
- Only after the object exists, apply per-segment colors or animations.
- Prefer this two-step flow unless the user explicitly asks for a different order.
